1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,200
A service is failing. Every second matters. The agent knows the rollback.
But capability is not permission. Who says yes, and can every step be proved? This is ArcadeOps.

2
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:26,800
Inside TrueForge, version forty-two is degraded. The mission: investigate, verify, then, only if every gate passes,
return checkout API to version forty-one. Authority Contract: one write. Nothing broader.

3
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:43,100
The agent inspects MCP state: 18.4% errors. TrueForge creates an independent, read-only Verifier.
It sees the same incident and plan, but cannot execute it. Two reads. Zero writes. Authority has not moved.

4
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:57,300
Next, a fail-closed validator runs in Daytona. It calls the MCP tools and checks service, versions, threshold,
and state-bound token. Without the exact SANDBOX_VALIDATION_PASS, the workflow stops.

5
00:00:57,300 --> 00:01:14,300
Then, silence. The rollback is ready, but irreversible. TrueForge pauses and shows the action.
The participant decides: Allow or Deny. Here, Allow was chosen; interface activation was delegated.
The click is not authority. The decision is.

6
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:29,700
One rollback executes. A fresh inspection finds version forty-one healthy, errors down to 0.7%.
Replays fail. Concurrent attempts produce one write. Anything out of scope returns Authority Denied.

7
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:44,700
Now, proof. The Evidence Console creates a portable receipt. Twenty-two checks correlate the reads, Verifier,
Daytona, approval, single write, and recovery. Change one required record, and the claim disappears.

8
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:58,099
ArcadeOps is not blind trust, and not fear of autonomy. It is relief. The agent moves at machine speed.
The person keeps the decision. And the evidence stays after both have left the room.
